            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="MPbzjftuWuTMiWZ8nj09A2" Bounds="1539,799,548,586" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="This is the most interesting part of the patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Here you can define which areas of the image are affected by the filter. This is done using a black and white texture.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The black and white texture acts as a control mask. White means the effect is applied, while black means the effect is transparent or not applied.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The control texture itself can also be filtered and distorted. In practice there is almost no limit to how many filters you can apply. These filters can also be rearranged within the processing chain.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In this example, the control mask starts as a circle. Its position is animated using the &quot;Wanderer&quot; node. Since white means &quot;affected&quot; and black means &quot;not affected&quot;, the colors are inverted to obtain the desired behaviour.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;An additional blur filter softens the texture so that the mask becomes grainy and less sharp.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Two nodes are particularly important:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ColorMap → Convert  &#xD;&#xA;This node allows the texture to be connected to the &quot;Control&quot; input of the Glitch node.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The datatype here is &quot;GPU Vector4&quot;. This is a four- dimensional vector that lives on the GPU and represents image data. Any node that accepts this datatype can receive the texture.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ValueMap works in a similar way. It converts values into a &quot;GPU Float&quot;, which is a single numeric value processed on the GPU.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This allows the output to be connected to parameters that expect GPU floats, such as the &quot;Strength&quot; or &quot;Amount&quot; inputs of many filters.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
        </Canvas>
